<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Crystal Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1851">1851</a>–<a href="/years/1936">1936</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Crystal Palace was a building of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cast-iron">cast iron</a> and glass designed by Joseph Paxton to house the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-exhibition">Great Exhibition</a> in Hyde Park, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in 1851. After the exhibition it was rebuilt in enlarged form at Sydenham in south London, where it served for exhibitions and entertainment until it was destroyed by fire in 1936.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-crystal-palace.jpg" alt="The Crystal Palace" width="320" height="243" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philip Henry Delamotte (1821–1889) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crystal_Palace_General_view_from_Water_Temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
